course: biology
lesson: 282
title: Comeback stories: bald eagles, humpback whales, condors and the kakapo
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Not every story ends in extinction: when people stop the harm and protect a kind of animal, it can come back, sometimes from just a few dozen.
explain: The bald eagle on American coins nearly vanished, because a pesticide called DDT made its eggshells so thin that they cracked when the parents sat on them; after DDT was banned in 1972 and eagles were protected, the numbers grew from about 400 pairs to over 70,000.
explain: Each comeback has its own trick: a hunting ban for whales, zoo breeding for condors and oryx, island homes with no predators for the kakapo, and brave rangers for mountain gorillas.
explain: These stories show that saving a species is hard and slow, but it really works.
fact: In the 1960s there were only about 400 nesting pairs of bald eagles in the lower 48 states of America, because DDT made their eggshells thin; after DDT was banned in 1972 and eagles were protected, there are now over 70,000 pairs.
fact: Humpback whales were hunted almost to extinction; after a ban on commercial whaling in 1986, many groups grew strongly, and there are now tens of thousands.
fact: Peregrine falcons also crashed because of DDT; people bred and released them, even in cities, where tall buildings are like cliffs and pigeons are their food.
fact: Sea otters were hunted for their fur almost to extinction, and protection brought them back along the Pacific coast; they eat sea urchins, which eat kelp, so otters protect kelp forests.
fact: In 2016 giant pandas moved from endangered, in great danger of dying out, to vulnerable, still at risk but less so; China protected bamboo forests and made corridors between them, and there are now about 1,800 or more in the wild.
fact: The Arabian oryx was extinct in the wild by 1972; zoos bred a herd and released them in Oman and other lands, and it was the first animal to go from extinct in the wild back to vulnerable.
fact: In 1987 only 27 California condors were left and all were taken into care; zoo breeding and release raised the number to over 500, but lead from hunters' bullets in dead animals still poisons them.
fact: The black-footed ferret was thought extinct until a dog found one in Wyoming in 1981; a breeding programme saved it, and in 2020 a ferret was cloned from cells frozen in 1988.
fact: Mountain gorillas fell to about 250 in the 1980s; rangers, local people and gorilla tourism helped them grow to over 1,000, and park rangers risk their lives to protect them.
fact: Red kites were wiped out in England by the late 1800s; birds from Spain were released from 1989, and now there are thousands; they eat mostly dead animals and are no danger to people.
fact: The kakapo is a fat, flightless parrot from New Zealand that is active at night; in 1995 only about 50 were left, all moved to islands without predators.
fact: Every kakapo has a name and is watched by rangers, and there are now about 250.
exercise: What made bald eagles nearly vanish? | DDT made their eggshells so thin that they cracked | Eagles forgot how to build nests | A disease killed all their chicks in one single year | Cold winters froze their eggs
exercise: When was DDT banned in America? | 1972 | 1872 | 2002 | 1932
exercise: How many pairs of bald eagles are there now in the lower 48 states? | Over 70,000 | Only about 400 | Only about 40 | Exactly 1,000
exercise: What helped humpback whales come back? | A ban on commercial whaling in 1986 | Feeding them fish every day in big zoos | Moving them into big freshwater lakes | Teaching them to jump higher than boats
exercise: Why do peregrine falcons like to nest on tall city buildings? | Buildings are like cliffs, and pigeons are their food | They like the warm air that blows out of the office heaters | They are afraid of trees | People leave them sweets
exercise: How do sea otters help kelp forests? | They eat sea urchins, which eat kelp | They plant kelp seeds on the sea floor | They clean kelp with stones every day | They eat the big sharks that bite the kelp
exercise: What did China do to help giant pandas? | Protected bamboo forests and made corridors between them | Moved all of its pandas into zoos abroad, far away from home | Taught pandas to eat meat | Built panda houses in cities
exercise: Which animal was the first to go from extinct in the wild to vulnerable? | The Arabian oryx | The giant panda of China | The kakapo of New Zealand | The red kite of Wales
exercise: How many California condors were left in 1987? | 27 | 270 | 2,700 | 7
exercise: What still poisons California condors? | Lead from hunters' bullets in dead animals | Plastic bags that blow about in the hot desert | Salt from the sea spray along the Pacific coast | Smoke from campfires in the forests at night
exercise: Who found the black-footed ferret again in 1981? | A dog in Wyoming | A satellite camera | A child in New York | A fisherman in Canada
exercise: What happened to a black-footed ferret in 2020? | One was cloned from cells frozen in 1988 | One flew to space | One was found living wild in Australia | One learnt to talk
exercise: What helped mountain gorillas grow to over 1,000? | Rangers, local people and gorilla tourism | Zoos taking them all away to Europe | Feeding them bananas every single day | Letting them live in towns
exercise: Where did the red kites released in England come from? | Spain | Australia | Canada | Japan
exercise: What kind of bird is the kakapo? | A fat, flightless parrot that is active at night | A tiny flying hummingbird that sips nectar | A kind of penguin | A big eagle
exercise: Where were all the kakapo moved to? | Islands without predators | Zoos in Europe and America | Cages in people's homes in cities | The high mountains of Nepal
test: «eagles on US money, so they've always been loads of them» What is right? | No, they fell to about 400 pairs before DDT was banned and they were protected | Yes, they were never in danger | Yes, they only live on coins | No, they are extinct now, so they only live on money as a way to remember them
test: Count them: 3 sea otters float on their backs, each with 1 stone on its belly. How many paws and stones are there in all? | 12 paws and 3 stones | 8 paws and 3 stones | 12 paws and 1 stone | 16 paws and 3 stones
test: «red kites are huge, they'll grab my dog» What is right? | Red kites eat mostly dead animals and are no danger to people or dogs | Yes, they swoop down and carry off small dogs and cats every single day | Yes, they eat cats too | No, red kites are only toys
test: What would happen to a kelp forest if all the sea otters were hunted again? | Sea urchins would multiply and eat much of the kelp | The kelp would grow faster than ever, with nothing to stop it | The urchins would all die | Nothing would change
test: «pandas are basically extinct, there's like ten left» What is right? | There are about 1,800 or more in the wild, and they are now listed as vulnerable | There are only ten left, all kept together in one zoo in the middle of China | Pandas are extinct in the wild | Pandas live only in America
test: In the film Up, a funny bird is chased by a dog. A real kakapo can't fly away either. How was it saved? | It was moved to islands with no predators and every bird is watched | It learnt to fly from other parrots that were kept in the same big cage for years | It was given wings by scientists | It hides in people's houses
test: «a condor ate a dead deer and got sick, how» What is the most likely reason? | Lead from a hunter's bullet in the deer poisoned it | The deer was too big and got stuck halfway down its throat | Condors cannot eat meat | It ate too fast
test: Count them: a ranger watches 2 mountain gorilla mums, each carrying 1 baby. How many gorillas, and how many arms in all? | 4 gorillas and 8 arms | 2 gorillas and 4 arms | 4 gorillas and 4 arms | 3 gorillas and 6 arms
test: «extinct in the wild means gone for ever, game over» What does the Arabian oryx story show? | Zoos bred them and released them, and they live in the wild again | It shows exactly that: once gone from the wild, the oryx can never come back | It shows oryx were never real | It shows oryx moved to the sea
test: «falcons live on cliffs, why's one on the hospital roof» What is right? | Tall buildings are like cliffs, and city pigeons are its food | It is lost and needs rescuing by the fire brigade before it falls | It is a robot falcon | Falcons only live in hospitals
test: «they cloned a ferret?? from what» What is right? | From cells frozen in 1988 | From a ferret's tooth found in a cave | From a toy ferret | From a cat's DNA
exam: Identify the principal cause of the decline of the bald eagle during the twentieth century. | The pesticide DDT, which caused the shells of its eggs to become thin | An epidemic of influenza among nestlings | The removal of eagles to zoological gardens | A succession of exceptionally cold winters
exam: State the measure principally responsible for the recovery of humpback whale populations. | The prohibition of commercial whaling in 1986 | The construction of artificial lakes | The feeding of whales by fishing vessels | The removal of all sharks from the oceans
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Arabian oryx. | It was the first animal to pass from the status of extinct in the wild to that of vulnerable | It has never been threatened with extinction | It became wholly extinct in 1972 and has not been seen since | It is confined exclusively to European zoos
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the protection of the kakapo? | The entire population was relocated to islands free of predators, each bird being named and monitored | The species was released into cities across Europe | The species was taught to fly by its keepers | The species is maintained solely in private homes
